// Stone to put on the ground.
//
//     import 'rocks' { Boulder, Scree }
//
//     Slope = path { move to -3, -3  line to 3, -3  line to 3, 3  line to -3, 3  close }
//
//     object Boulder(1.2)
//     object Scree(Slope, 'winter')
//
// The `trees` and `undergrowth` libraries grow things.  This one is what they grow among, and it is
// the last thing a piece of ground needs before it stops looking swept.
//
// | `Boulder` | One big weathered stone, lumpy all over.  The expensive one -- see below. |
// | `Cobble` | A smaller stone with flat faces, knocked off something bigger.  Cheap. |
// | `Scree` | An area of cobbles, thrown down thickly, filling any outline you draw. |
// | `MossyBoulder` | A boulder with moss on its top and its shaded side, in patches. |
// | `MossyCobble` | A cobble capped with moss. |
// | `DryStoneWall` | A wall of stones laid without mortar, battered in toward its coping of stones on edge. |
// | `Rubble` | A heap of broken stone, whole stones tumbled on one another. |
//
// The first three arguments mean what they mean in the other libraries -- **how big**, **what time of
// year**, and **which one of that kind** -- and only the first is ever required.  The mossy two take a
// fourth, the way their shaded side faces, which the moss creeps furthest down.  For `Boulder` and
// `Cobble` the size is roughly how far across the stone is; for `Scree` it is the outline the stones
// fill, since an area has a shape as well as a size and a heap of stone is never square.
//
// **The season does one thing here, and it is winter.**  A rock is not deciduous, so three seasons of
// the four are the same stone; in winter snow lies on top of it, in the same way it gathers on the fir
// in `trees` and the boxwood in `undergrowth`.  That is the whole of what the word does, and it is
// asked for anyway so that a scene setting its season in one place gets rock that agrees with the
// grass around it.
//
// -- Two kinds of stone, and why there are two --------------------------------------------------
//
// A rock is a shape problem before it is anything else: what gives a stone away instantly is a
// silhouette that is too regular.  A sphere reads as a ball from every angle, and no amount of
// coloring the surface repairs it, because the outline is what the eye checks.
//
// So `Boulder` is an **isosurface** -- a ball with noise subtracted from its radius, which makes an
// outline that is irregular in the way stone is.  It is the real thing and it costs the real price:
// an isosurface is found by marching along the ray rather than solved for, and sixty-four of them
// measured about twenty times what sixty-four spheres cost.
//
// `Cobble` is a **sphere with flat faces cut off it** by a couple of turned cubes -- everything
// analytic, nothing marched.  It reads as broken stone rather than weathered stone, which is what a
// smaller rock usually is anyway, and it measured about four times a sphere rather than twenty.
//
// The division is the same one `undergrowth` makes between `Tuft` and `Grass`: the expensive, careful
// one for the few you look at, and the cheap one for the many you do not.  A picture wants some of
// each -- a boulder or two among a scree of cobbles -- and that is what the sizes are chosen for.
//
// -- What is here for the library and not for you ------------------------------------------------
//
// Everything beginning with `Rock` is the machinery.  The materials arrive in your scene whether you
// asked or not, for the reason the other libraries give -- a material is looked up where it is *used*
// -- so they carry the prefix and will not collide with anything of yours.

// -- Stone ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Mottled rather than flat, and the mottling is what saves the whole library from looking like putty.
// A pigment is worked out from where a point is *in the scene*, so two stones side by side take
// different parts of the same speckle and come out looking like two stones rather than one repeated.
//
// **The stops are placed where granite's values actually fall, and the first version of this was not.**
// It is natural to write a color map across nought to one, and quite wrong here: granite is a sum of
// six octaves of `|0.5 - noise|`, and measured over sixty thousand points it runs 0.017 to 0.684 with
// a mean of 0.232 and **99.2% of it below 0.5**.  Stops at 0.0, 0.5 and 1.0 therefore squeezed every
// stone into the gap between the first two colors: an intended spread of 0.26 to 0.49 came out as
// 0.28 to 0.36, a third of the contrast that was written and all of it in the pale middle.  Rocks that
// each had a single flat tone, which is most of what made them look like putty after all.
//
// These stops are the 1st, 25th, 50th, 75th and 95th percentiles of what granite really gives, so the
// whole of the color range gets used and the speckle arrives.
//
// **And they are darker than they look like they should be.**  Dry granite sits near a fifth of the
// light it receives, and basalt lower; the old middle tone of 0.32 was closer to concrete.  Under a
// warm daylight sky a too-bright gray does not read as pale stone, it reads as pale *plastic*, and
// takes the sun's color with it -- which is where the yellow came from.
RockGray = pigment granite with seed 7 {
    [0.06, color [0.085, 0.082, 0.078],
     0.16, color [0.135, 0.130, 0.122],
     0.22, color [0.185, 0.178, 0.166],
     0.30, color [0.245, 0.236, 0.220],
     0.41, color [0.325, 0.313, 0.292]]
    scale 0.35
}

// **Rock has almost no highlight, and what it has is not gathered.**  A tenth of specular at a
// shininess of 22 is a soft, collected sheen, and a collected sheen sliding over a smoothly shaded
// form is the strongest plastic cue there is -- it says *coated*.  Stone is scattering light off a
// surface rough at a scale far below anything modeled here, so what it gives back is broad and dim.
// The ambient comes down with it: a sixth of the light arriving from nowhere in particular was lifting
// the crevices to the same tone as the faces, which flattens exactly the shading that says *rough*.
RockStone = material {
    pigment RockGray
    specular 0.03
    shininess 9
    ambient 0.06
    brilliance 1.4
}
RockSnow = material { pigment [0.93, 0.94, 0.97]  specular 0.28  shininess 55 }

// What lies on top of a stone, which is nothing at all for three seasons of the four.
//
// It is a handful of squashed balls **sunk into** the top of the rock, most of each one inside the
// stone so that only its crown shows.  Two earlier tries were worse in instructive ways.  One big ball
// sat on top reads as a mushroom.  One big ball sunk in reads as a dish of cream, because a smooth
// dome laid across a rough stone announces that the two were made by different hands.  Snow scattered
// in patches breaks that line up, and it is the same thing the boxwood in `undergrowth` does with the
// snow on its crown.
primitive RockCap(season, size, flat, variant) -> group {
    switch (season) {
        case 'winter' {
            return group {
                // Whether this stone caught any snow at all is decided first, and about a third do
                // not.  Snow lies unevenly -- it blows off, it slides, it melts where the stone
                // faces the sun -- and a scree in which *every* stone wears a white cap reads as a
                // field of eggs.  A third of them bare is what makes the rest look like weather.
                if (random(variant, 65) > 0.34) {
                    for patch in [0, 6] {
                        if (random(patch, 62, variant) > 0.34) {
                            sphere {
                                material RockSnow
                                scale [size * 0.30, size * flat * 0.26, size * 0.30]
                                translate X size * 0.50 * random(patch, 63, variant)
                                rotate Y patch * 53 + random(patch, 64, variant) * 44
                                translate Y size * flat * 0.60
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        default { return group { } }
    }
}

// -- Boulder -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A ball with noise taken out of its radius, at two scales: a coarse one that decides the lumps and a
// finer one that roughens what the coarse one left.
//
// The noise is sampled at an offset that comes from the variant, which is the only way one of these
// can be told from another -- `random` cannot be called inside a field, since a field is compiled
// once and asked about millions of points, so the variation has to be carried in rather than drawn
// there.  Moving where the noise is *read* is as good as changing the noise.
//
// `bounded by` is a little wider than the ball, because the noise pushes the surface out past radius
// one and anything outside the box is simply not there -- a box drawn too tight slices the stone off
// flat rather than failing in any way that says so.
primitive Boulder(size, season = 'summer', variant = 1) -> group {
    ox = random(variant, 11) * 40
    oy = random(variant, 12) * 40
    oz = random(variant, 13) * 40
    flat = 0.58 + random(variant, 14) * 0.22

    return group {
        isosurface {
            material RockStone
            function {
                sqrt(x * x + y * y + z * z) - 1
                    - 0.23 * (noise(x * 1.9 + ox, y * 1.9 + oy, z * 1.9 + oz) - 0.5)
                    - 0.11 * (noise(x * 4.3 + ox, y * 4.3 + oy, z * 4.3 + oz) - 0.5)
                    - 0.05 * (noise(x * 9.1 + ox, y * 9.1 + oy, z * 9.1 + oz) - 0.5)
            }
            bounded by [-1.6, -1.6, -1.6], [1.6, 1.6, 1.6]
            scale [size * 0.5, size * 0.5 * flat, size * 0.5 * (0.82 + random(variant, 15) * 0.3)]
            rotate Y random(variant, 16) * 360
        }

        object RockCap(season, size * 0.5, flat, variant)
    }
}

// -- Cobble --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A stone knocked off a bigger one: a ball with two flat faces cut across it.  Everything here is
// analytic -- one sphere and two cubes -- so it costs what a handful of spheres costs rather than
// what a marched surface costs.
//
// The cubes have to be *smaller* than the ball to cut it at all.  A cube wider than the sphere it is
// intersected with removes nothing, which is a mistake that looks like the cutting not working.
primitive Cobble(size, season = 'summer', variant = 1) -> group {
    flat = 0.55 + random(variant, 21) * 0.25

    return group {
        intersection {
            sphere { }
            cube {
                scale 0.88
                rotate X (random(variant, 22) - 0.5) * 80
                rotate Y random(variant, 23) * 360
                translate [(random(variant, 24) - 0.5) * 0.45, 0,
                           (random(variant, 25) - 0.5) * 0.45]
            }
            cube {
                scale 0.82
                rotate Z (random(variant, 26) - 0.5) * 80
                rotate Y random(variant, 27) * 360
                translate [0, (random(variant, 28) - 0.5) * 0.45,
                           (random(variant, 29) - 0.5) * 0.45]
            }
            material RockStone
            scale [size * 0.5, size * 0.5 * flat, size * 0.5 * (0.8 + random(variant, 30) * 0.35)]
            rotate Y random(variant, 31) * 360
        }

        object RockCap(season, size * 0.5, flat, variant)
    }
}

// -- Moss ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Cushion moss: a deep green, its fronds too fine and too many to shine.
RockMoss = material { pigment color [0.17, 0.29, 0.07]  roughness 0.55 }

// A boulder grown over with moss.
//
// The moss is hair grown over the stone: a centimeter and a half of it, its fronds thicker than real
// ones and fewer, so that it covers the stone as a cushion does without costing millions of strands.
// It grows where the stone faces up, and down its shaded side further than its sunny one -- `shade`
// is the way that side faces -- and only in patches, which differ from one variant to the next.  Rain
// runs off the steep places and the sun dries the open ones; a stone mossed evenly all over reads as a
// stone painted green.
//
// **It is grown over the bare stone and the snow is put on afterward**, so that the moss lies under the
// snow rather than growing over it.
primitive MossyBoulder(size, season = 'summer', variant = 1, shade = [0, 0, 1]) -> group {
    flat = 0.58 + random(variant, 14) * 0.22

    return group {
        object Boulder(size, 'summer', variant) {
            hair {
                density 150000  length 0.015  thickness 0.006  vary 0.4  lean 35  frizz 0.5
                patches bozo with seed variant { poly 2 wave  scale 0.3 }
                facing vector [0, 1, 0] + (vector shade) * 0.6 by 1.2
                material RockMoss
            }
        }
        object RockCap(season, size * 0.5, flat, variant)
    }
}

// A cobble grown over with moss, as the boulder is.
primitive MossyCobble(size, season = 'summer', variant = 1, shade = [0, 0, 1]) -> group {
    flat = 0.55 + random(variant, 21) * 0.25

    return group {
        object Cobble(size, 'summer', variant) {
            hair {
                density 150000  length 0.015  thickness 0.006  vary 0.4  lean 35  frizz 0.5
                patches bozo with seed variant { poly 2 wave  scale 0.3 }
                facing vector [0, 1, 0] + (vector shade) * 0.6 by 1.2
                material RockMoss
            }
        }
        object RockCap(season, size * 0.5, flat, variant)
    }
}

// -- Scree ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// One stone of a scree, lifted so that it sits *into* the ground rather than on it.  The lift goes
// with the stone, so a field that sizes the stone sizes its lift with it.
primitive RockScreeStone(n, season, variant, size) -> group {
    return group {
        object Cobble(size, season, variant * 6421 + n) { translate Y size * 0.16 }
    }
}

// An area of cobbles, filling whatever outline it is given.  The outline is read on the X/Z plane, so
// a scree is the shape you draw, lying where you draw it.
//
// The stones stand on a grid and are then thrown nearly a whole cell off it, exactly as the grass is
// and for the same reason: a lattice is very easy to notice and impossible to unsee.  Eight stones are
// built and every place stands one of them, turned any way and sized from about half the size asked for
// to a third over it -- the same arrangement `Grass` uses, and for the same saving.
//
// **This was a square, and squares are why a heap of stone never sat against anything.**  Scree runs
// out where a slope runs out; it does not stop along a line at right angles to another line.  It was
// also gathered by hand into blocks of sixty-four, which used to be what made it affordable -- a group
// that a ray got inside asked *every* child.  A group arranges its own children now, so a field of
// them costs what the blocking used to and says a great deal less.
primitive Scree(shape, season = 'summer', variant = 1, size = 0.3, density = 1) -> field {
    return field {
        of RockScreeStone(0, season, variant, size), RockScreeStone(1, season, variant, size),
           RockScreeStone(2, season, variant, size), RockScreeStone(3, season, variant, size),
           RockScreeStone(4, season, variant, size), RockScreeStone(5, season, variant, size),
           RockScreeStone(6, season, variant, size), RockScreeStone(7, season, variant, size)
        within shape
        spacing size * 1.25 / density
        jitter 1.7
        turned 360
        sized 0.55 to 1.3
    }
}

// -- Walls and rubble ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Stone that has been worked by hand, or brought down: a wall laid dry, and the heap a wall becomes.
// Both are a solid broken into pieces by a `fracture` -- every stone the part of the solid nearest one
// seed, so they fit together as laid stone does, each worn round at its edges and turned a little.
//
// **Four stones, not one.**  A wall is laid from whatever the field gave up, so no two stones are quite
// the same color: gray, buff, blue-gray and brown, all as dark as real stone is (see the stone above --
// a too-pale gray reads as plastic), and each stone in a place of its own in its grain (`loose`), so the
// speckle of one does not run on into the next and give the wall away as one block cracked.

RockWallGray = material {
    pigment granite with seed 7 {
    [0.06, color [0.102, 0.098, 0.094],
     0.16, color [0.162, 0.156, 0.146],
     0.22, color [0.222, 0.214, 0.199],
     0.30, color [0.294, 0.283, 0.264],
     0.41, color [0.390, 0.376, 0.350]]
    scale 0.09
    }
    specular 0.03  shininess 9  ambient 0.06  brilliance 1.4
}
RockWallBuff = material {
    pigment granite with seed 7 {
    [0.06, color [0.114, 0.102, 0.084],
     0.16, color [0.181, 0.162, 0.132],
     0.22, color [0.249, 0.222, 0.179],
     0.30, color [0.329, 0.295, 0.238],
     0.41, color [0.437, 0.391, 0.315]]
    scale 0.09
    }
    specular 0.03  shininess 9  ambient 0.06  brilliance 1.4
}
RockWallBlue = material {
    pigment granite with seed 7 {
    [0.06, color [0.095, 0.096, 0.099],
     0.16, color [0.151, 0.153, 0.155],
     0.22, color [0.206, 0.209, 0.211],
     0.30, color [0.273, 0.278, 0.280],
     0.41, color [0.363, 0.368, 0.371]]
    scale 0.09
    }
    specular 0.03  shininess 9  ambient 0.06  brilliance 1.4
}
RockWallBrown = material {
    pigment granite with seed 7 {
    [0.06, color [0.112, 0.095, 0.082],
     0.16, color [0.178, 0.151, 0.129],
     0.22, color [0.244, 0.207, 0.175],
     0.30, color [0.323, 0.275, 0.232],
     0.41, color [0.429, 0.364, 0.308]]
    scale 0.09
    }
    specular 0.03  shininess 9  ambient 0.06  brilliance 1.4
}

// The hearting: the small stone packed into the middle of a dry wall between its two faces.  It is dark,
// because it is in shadow: a wall without it is a sieve, daylight showing through every joint, and its
// joints come out pale where they should be the darkest thing on it.
RockHearting = material { pigment [0.05, 0.048, 0.045]  specular 0  ambient 0.02 }

// What lies along a wall's top in winter: snow on the coping, in lumps, some stones bare.
primitive RockWallSnow(season, length, height, top, variant) -> group {
    switch (season) {
        case 'winter' {
            lumps = floor(length / 0.3)

            return group {
                for lump in [0, lumps - 1] {
                    if (random(lump, 71, variant) > 0.25) {
                        sphere {
                            material RockSnow
                            scale [0.20 + random(lump, 72, variant) * 0.08, 0.045, top + 0.02]
                            translate [(lump + 0.5) * length / lumps - length / 2, height + 0.25, 0]
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        default { return group { } }
    }
}

// A dry stone wall, its length along X, standing on the ground at its middle, its faces toward -Z and
// +Z.  It is wider at the foot than the top -- battered, so it leans in on itself and stands without
// mortar -- and topped with a row of stones set on edge across it, the coping, which is what keeps the
// rain out of it and what anybody would draw first.
//
// The stones are longer than they are tall, and set lengthwise along the wall, which is the one rule a
// waller keeps: the body is broken stretched along X and across it, so its stones come out in rough
// courses, about as many as there would be in a wall of that size.  The coping's are stretched across
// the wall instead, standing on edge.
//
// Its height is what you would measure to the top of the coping; its length is its length.
primitive DryStoneWall(length, height = 1.2, season = 'summer', variant = 1) -> group {
    // Half the width at the foot, half the width at the top of the body under the coping.
    base = 0.32
    top = 0.20
    body = height - 0.24
    batter = atan2(base - top, body) * 180 / 3.14159265

    return group {
        fracture {
            solid {
                intersection {
                    cube { scale [length / 2, body / 2, base]  translate [0, body / 2, 0] }
                    cube { scale [length, body * 2, base]  rotate X -batter }
                    cube { scale [length, body * 2, base]  rotate X batter }
                }
            }
            pieces length * body * (base + top) / 0.009
            gap 0.02
            rounded 0.025
            stretched [2.2, 1, 1.3]
            tumble 5
            loose
            materials RockWallGray, RockWallBuff, RockWallBlue, RockWallBrown
            with seed variant
        }

        // The hearting, set back from both faces.
        intersection {
            cube { scale [length / 2 - 0.02, body / 2, base - 0.07]  translate [0, body / 2, 0] }
            cube { scale [length, body * 2, base - 0.07]  rotate X -batter }
            cube { scale [length, body * 2, base - 0.07]  rotate X batter }
            material RockHearting
        }

        // The coping.
        fracture {
            solid { cube { scale [length / 2, 0.12, top + 0.03]  translate [0, body + 0.12, 0] } }
            pieces length / 0.13
            gap 0.016
            rounded 0.03
            flat
            stretched [1, 1, 4]
            tumble 9
            loose
            materials RockWallGray, RockWallBuff, RockWallBlue, RockWallBrown
            with seed variant + 1
        }

        object RockWallSnow(season, length, body, top, variant)
    }
}

// A heap of rubble: broken stone tumbled into a low mound, its size how far across it is.
//
// **Whole stones, not a mound cut into pieces.**  A heap broken from a smooth mound would wear the mound's
// smooth skin on every outer stone; these are broken from the space round it (`whole`), and kept where
// their middles fall inside the mound, so the outline is made of whole stones lying where they fell.
primitive Rubble(size, season = 'summer', variant = 1) -> group {
    return group {
        fracture {
            solid { sphere { scale [size * 0.5, size * 0.22, size * 0.42] } }
            pieces 60
            gap size * 0.02
            rounded size * 0.018
            tumble 25
            whole
            loose
            materials RockWallGray, RockWallBuff, RockWallBlue, RockWallBrown
            with seed variant
        }

        object RockCap(season, size * 0.5, 0.44, variant)
    }
}
